      "text": "First, we start with the given polynomial equation \\(x^3 - 5x^2 + 6x - 9 = 0\\). The solutions to this equation are \\(r\\), \\(s\\), and \\(t\\). By Vieta's formulas, we know the following relationships:\n\n1. \\(r + s + t = 5\\)\n2. \\(rs + rt + st = 6\\)\n3. \\(rst = 9\\)\n\nWe need to compute the expression \\(\\frac{rs}{t} + \\frac{st}{r} + \\frac{tr}{s}\\).\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the expression in a common denominator form:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{rs}{t} + \\frac{st}{r} + \\frac{tr}{s} = \\frac{rs \\cdot rs + st \\cdot tr + tr \\cdot st}{rst} = \\frac{(rs)^2 + (st)^2 + (tr)^2}{rst}\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the identity for the sum of squares of the roots in terms of the sum and product of the roots:\n\n\\[\n(rs)^2 + (st)^2 + (tr)^2 = (rs + st + tr)^2 - 2rst(r + s + t)\n\\]\n\nWe already know \\(rs + st + tr = 6\\) and \\(rst = 9\\). Substituting these values into the identity, we get:\n\n\\[\n(rs)^2 + (st)^2 + (tr)^2 = 6^2 - 2 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 5 = 36 - 90 = -54\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute \\((rs)^2 + (st)^2 + (tr)^2 = -54\\) and \\(rst = 9\\) into the expression \\(\\frac{(rs)^2 + (st)^2 + (tr)^2}{rst}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(rs)^2 + (st)^2 + (tr)^2}{rst} = \\frac{-54}{9} = -6\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-6}\n\\]",
